// test_regalloc_rpo.bpp — RPO sidequest verification fixture (RegAlloc
// v2, Stage C's prerequisite). Functions named __rg_rpo_* trigger
// bpp_regalloc.bsm's RPO debug dump at compile time
// (regalloc_name_wants_rpo_dump) — this file's PURPOSE is the
// compiler's own stdout while compiling it, captured and checked by
// tests/test_regalloc_rpo.sh.

// The exact shape that breaks allocation-order linearization (see
// regalloc_compute_rpo's own comment in bpp_regalloc.bsm): a while
// loop whose body contains an if whose body contains its OWN while
// loop. The outer loop's continue-block is allocated BEFORE its body
// is built, so the inner while's blocks (built while processing that
// body) get LARGER allocation indices — yet the inner while's
// surrounding if-join falls through to that already-allocated,
// lower-indexed continue block at the end of every outer iteration: a
// real edge from a higher allocation index to a lower one, with no
// loop or back-edge involved.
//
// Block structure (confirmed via the Stage A CFG dump on this exact
// function, by hand before writing this test):
//   0=entry  1=outer_header  2=outer_body(if-cond)  3=outer_exit
//   4=outer_continue  5=if_entry  6=inner_header  7=inner_body
//   8=inner_exit(if_exit)  9=inner_continue  10=if_join  11=tail
// Edges (ignoring the two real back-edges, 9->6 and 4->1):
//   0->1, 1->2, 1->3, 2->5, 2->10, 5->6, 6->7, 6->8, 7->9, 8->10, 10->4.
// RPO must place every edge's source before its target — in
// particular 10 before 4, even though 4's ALLOCATION index (4) is
// lower than 10's.
__rg_rpo_nested(c, x, y) -> word {
    auto r;
    r = 0;
    while (c > 0) {
        if (x > 0) {
            while (y > 0) {
                r = r + 1;
                y = y - 1;
            }
        }
        c = c - 1;
    }
    return r;
}

main() {
    auto r;
    // y is NOT reset between outer iterations, so only the FIRST
    // outer iteration's inner loop actually runs (y reaches 0 and
    // stays there) — r ends at 3, not 6. (Caught by hand-tracing the
    // arithmetic with put() before writing this assertion — first
    // draft of this test expected 6 and failed, which was this
    // test's own bug, not the compiler's.)
    r = __rg_rpo_nested(2, 1, 3);
    if (r != 3) { return 1; }
    return 0;
}
